mer","url":"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/3961#section=3D-Conformer"},"properties":{"weight":422.9,"xlogp":4.3,"hbd":2,"hba":5,"tpsa":92.5},"brandList":[{"name":"Cosaar","url":"https://medikamente.basg.gv.at/documents/1-28585__DOTC_FACH_INFO.pdf"}],"product":{"label":"Cosaar 50 mg film-coated tablets","url":"https://medikamente.basg.gv.at/documents/1-28585__DOTC_FACH_INFO.pdf","source":"BASG"},"groups":[{"ruleId":"tetrazole","name":"Tetrazole","atoms":[4,5,6,7,28],"ring":[28,4,6,7,5],"count":1,"term":"bioisostere","note":{"caption":"The tetrazole stands in for a carboxylic acid: similar acidity, more stable.","body":["In losartan's design, an earlier lead carried a carboxylic acid that bound well but was metabolically unstable. A tetrazole was chosen instead because its acidity is close to that of a carboxylic acid, its flat four-nitrogen ring can interact with positive charges in the receptor, and it resists metabolism. This makes it a classic acidic bioisostere."],"sources":[{"citation":"Yuan Y, Li M, Apostolopoulos V, et al. Tetrazoles: a multi-potent motif in drug design. Eur J Med Chem. 2024;279:116870","url":"https://vuir.vu.edu.au/49281/","licence":"CC BY 4.0"},{"citation":"ChEBI. Losartan (CHEBI:6541). European Bioinformatics Institute","url":"https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/searchId.do?chebiId=CHEBI:6541","licence":"CC BY 4.0"}],"review":{"status":"unreviewed"}}},{"ruleId":"imidazole","name":"Imidazole","atoms":[2,3,8,12,15],"ring":[8,2,12,15,3],"count":1,"note":{"caption":"A substituted imidazole: the part later sartans most often swapped out.","body":["The imidazole carries three substituents: a butyl chain, a chlorine and a hydroxymethyl group. A methylene bridge joins it to the biphenyl. When chemists designed later sartans, most changes to losartan's skeleton replaced this imidazole with other heteroaromatic or open-chain groups, while the biphenyl and its acidic group were kept."],"sources":[{"citation":"ChEBI. Losartan (CHEBI:6541). European Bioinformatics Institute","url":"https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/searchId.do?chebiId=CHEBI:6541","licence":"CC BY 4.0"},{"citation":"Rapposelli S, Cuboni S, Digiacomo M, et al. Synthesis and AT1 affinity evaluation of benzamidophenyl analogs of known AT1 receptor ligands with similar aromatic skeleton. ARKIVOC. 2008;(ii):268-286","url":"https://arkat-usa.org/get-file/23351","licence":"Free full text"}],"review":{"status":"unreviewed"}}},{"ruleId":"biphenyl","name":"Biphenyl","atoms":[13,17,18,19,20,21,23,24,25,26,27,29],"ring":[],"count":1,"note":{"caption":"The biphenyl is the shared sartan scaffold that carries the acidic group.","body":["Losartan is classed as a biphenylyltetrazole: the tetrazole sits on one ring of a biphenyl, next to the bond joining the two rings. Sartans share this layout, a biphenyl bearing an acidic group and linked through a methylene to a heterocyclic or open-chain unit."],"sources":[{"citation":"ChEBI. Losartan (CHEBI:6541). European Bioinformatics Institute","url":"https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/searchId.do?chebiId=CHEBI:6541","licence":"CC BY 4.0"},{"citation":"Rapposelli S, Cuboni S, Digiacomo M, et al. Synthesis and AT1 affinity evaluation of benzamidophenyl analogs of known AT1 receptor ligands with similar aromatic skeleton. ARKIVOC. 2008;(ii):268-286","url":"https://arkat-usa.org/get-file/23351","licence":"Free full text"}],"review":{"status":"unreviewed"}}},{"ruleId":"alcohol","name":"Hydroxyl group","atoms":[1,16],"ring":[],"count":1,"term":"hydrogen-bond-donor","note":{"caption":"The hydroxymethyl group is oxidised to a carboxylic acid, giving EXP3174.","body":["The primary alcohol on the imidazole ring is a metabolic handle. Oxidation turns it into a carboxylic acid, producing losartan carboxylic acid (EXP3174). This metabolite is reported to be 10 to 40 times more potent than losartan and accounts for most of its activity."],"sources":[{"citation":"Rapposelli S, Cuboni S, Digiacomo M, et al. Synthesis and AT1 affinity evaluation of benzamidophenyl analogs of known AT1 receptor ligands with similar aromatic skeleton. ARKIVOC. 2008;(ii):268-286","url":"https://arkat-usa.org/get-file/23351","licence":"Free full text"},{"citation":"ChEBI. Losartan carboxylic acid (CHEBI:74125). European Bioinformatics Institute","url":"https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/searchId.do?chebiId=CHEBI:74125","licence":"CC BY 4.0"}],"review":{"status":"unreviewed"}}},{"ruleId":"aryl_chloride","name":"Aryl chloride","atoms":[0,15],"ring":[],"count":1,"note":{"caption":"The chlorine sits on the imidazole ring, so it is strictly a heteroaryl chloride.","body":["Losartan's only chlorine is attached at position 4 of the aromatic imidazole ring, not to a benzene ring. It is carried unchanged into the active metabolite, which ChEBI also classes as an organochlorine compound."],"sources":[{"citation":"ChEBI. Losartan (CHEBI:6541). European Bioinformatics Institute","url":"https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/searchId.do?chebiId=CHEBI:6541","licence":"CC BY 4.0"},{"citation":"ChEBI. Losartan carboxylic acid (CHEBI:74125). European Bioinformatics Institute","url":"https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/searchId.do?chebiId=CHEBI:74125","licence":"CC BY 4.0"}],"review":{"status":"unreviewed"}}}],"pubchemUrl":"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/3961"}